 * ``1`` if the first version is greater than the second version.
 
 
+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to sort. All of them must be valid semver strings.
+    If none are given, versions are read from *standard in*, one per
+    line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text, one
+version per line, which makes the output easy to consume in a shell
+loop::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 | while read v; do echo "release $v"; done
+    release 1.2.0
+    release 1.10.0
+
+If a version is invalid, an error message is printed and nothing is
+sorted. Without any version (arguments or stdin), nothing is printed.
+Versions can be piped in::
+
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to search. All of them must be valid semver strings.
+    If none are given, versions are read from *standard in*, one per
+    line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The result is printed on *standard out* as a JSON string::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc.1 1.10.0
+    "1.10.0"
+
+If there is no version, or all versions are prereleases, an error
+message is printed and the return code is 2.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
